+(duration)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. A value is either a whole
+number of seconds, such as `120`, or one or more `<integer><unit>` groups using the units `d` (days), `h` (hours), `m`
+(minutes), and `s` (seconds), such as `2d`, `1h30m`, or `1d2h3m4s`. Each unit may appear at most once, units must be in
+that order, and units are case-insensitive. Spaces, signs, and decimals are not allowed. A {class}`datetime.timedelta`
+passed as a default or from Python is returned unchanged.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s")
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds.")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=120'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h 30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--help'])
+```
+
+```{versionadded} 8.6
+```
+
